# Li - Ugur interatomic potential, CANDIDATE, re-cut, switched
#
# pair_style ugur
# pair_coeff * * Li_recut.ugur Li
#
# Truncation: switched from 0.85 of each cutoff to the cutoff, quintic, C2 - energy is conserved in MD.  CANDIDATE, not part of the published library.  Against measured neutron dispersion this arm reaches 8.3 % over the 16 elements that have both a candidate record and a neutron curve and were not rejected outright, against 11.2 % for the published switched arm on the same 16.  That gap is almost all alkali: sodium, potassium, rubidium and caesium are where the arm gains most and are also what it breaks warm.  Leave them out and it is 8.3 % against 8.9 % over 11 elements, better in 7 - a wash.  There is no measured reason to prefer this arm for its dispersion
# MD: structure screened - holds its shape at 600 K (349 K, as equipartition requires).  This tested the SHAPE, not energy conservation; for that see the truncation line above.
#
# Lattice stability: screened on the 8^3 and 9^3 meshes AND along the
# symmetry path; no imaginary modes.
#
# Nudge test: passed - displaced by 1e-5 A and relaxed, the lattice returns.
#
# Measured dispersion: 5.8 % against 25.2 % for the published switched
# arm - better.
# Read that beside the finite-temperature note below rather than instead of
# it.  Both are the same fact: a dispersion at 0 K is a property of the
# curvature AT the minimum, and this arm has that neighbourhood right
# while the shape of the well away from it is wrong.
#
# FINITE TEMPERATURE: this candidate passes the screens above and fails
# below room temperature - thermal expansion comes out NEGATIVE (-37e-6/K against a measured 46); C11 RISES 359 % between 0 K and 300 K instead of softening.
# Use it for static properties only, if at all.
#
# Fitted to: cohesive energy, lattice constant, bulk modulus and the elastic
# constants of Li at its experimental lattice constant.  Nothing else.
#
# Format is LAMMPS's Tersoff convention - one line per ordered triple
# (centre, leg, leg) - so that this file and a multi-element one are read the
# same way.  For a single element that is one line.
#
# el1 el2 el3  m D alpha r0 gamma C alpha3 rcut2 rcut3 taper lam2 lam4
